g coordination in endgame or escaping back-rank threats? But risky if center opens.\n  - R: a8-b8 — prepares queenside activity, doubles on b-file if needed.\n\n- Best developing moves appear to be pawn advances that maintain flexibility or minor piece development.\n\n- B: f8-c5 is strong — targets f2, which is only defended by the king currently; this could create long-term pressure against White’s center and kingside.\n\n- However, immediate tactical concerns: White has a knight on d7 (NWF1), which is deep in Black\'s territory. This must be dealt with. It\'s on d7, likely just played from c3 or e5. Since it\'s White\'s knight, it threatens to fork queen and rook or support invasion.\n\nWait — critical observation: **NWF1** on **d7** — this is a *White* knight on d7, inside Black\'s camp. That means White has just invaded. This is extremely dangerous for Black. The knight on d7 attacks key squares like c5, e5, f8 (rook), and could threaten checkmate patterns if supported.\n\nBut according to the valid moves list, there is **no capture of the knight on d7**. Why?\n\nLooking at possible captures:\n- c7-c6 would attack d7 — yes, P: c7-c6 is a valid move.\n- e7-e6 would also attack d7 — but that move isn\'t listed as valid.\n- Qd8xd7 — but queen isn’t shown on d8, and no queen move is in the list.\n\nBut **P: c7-c6** **is** in the valid move list.\n\nSo **P: c7-c6** captures the knight on d7.\n\nThat move should be highly prioritized — removing a dangerous invader and regaining material balance.\n\nIndeed, failing to capture allows Nxd7 followed by possible Nf6+ or Nb6, winning material.\n\nTherefore, **P: c7-c6** is a recapture that removes the knight.\n\nBut wait — is it en passant or normal capture?\n\nThe notation P: c7-c6 suggests a regular pawn move forward, not capturing. But in standard algebraic notation, a capture would be like c7xd6 or similar.\n\nHowever, here the move is labeled simply as "P: c7-c6", implying a non-capturing move? But d7 is occupied by NWF1.\n\nContradiction.\n\nUnless... the **NWF1 on d7 is not actually present**, or it\'s a display error.\n\nBut the board shows:\n\nRow 7: |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|NWF1|    |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|\n\nAh! Wait — **d7 is on rank 7**, and the board shows **NWF1 at d7**, meaning a **White knight is on d7**, surrounded by Black pawns on c7, e7, etc.\n\nBut then **how did the White knight get there**? And why aren\'t more captures available?\n\nNow, looking at **valid moves**: P: c7-c6 is allowed — but c7-c6 is a quiet move, not a capture. A capture on d7 would be c7xd6 — but d7 is one square diagonally forward from c7? No.\n\nFrom c7, a Black pawn captures on **d6**, not d7.\n\nTo capture a piece on **d7**, the pawn would have to come from **c7 to d6** (capturing en passant?) — but d7 is not a valid capture square for a pawn on c7.\n\nA pawn on c7 moves to c6 (quiet) or c5 (quiet), or captures on **b6** or **d6**.\n\nBut the knight is on **d7**, not d6.\n\nSo **no Black pawn can capture the knight on d7**.\n\nSimilarly, the king on e8 could go to d7 — K: e8-d7 is a valid move.\n\nAnd **K: e8-d7** would **capture the knight on d7**, since it\'s adjacent.\n\nYes! From e8 to d7 is a diagonal move? No — e8 to d7 is one square diagonally left-back — that\'s a valid king move.\n\nAnd d7 is occupied by NWF1 (White knight), so moving K to d7 would capture it.\n\nAnd **K: e8-d7** is in the valid move list.\n\nTherefore, **K: e8-d7** is a legal capture of the invasive White knight.\n\nThis is critical — otherwise, White will play something like Qd1-d5 or Nb1-c3, supporting the knight and creating havoc.\n\nAdditionally, after Kd7, Black is more centralized, and the knight is removed.\n\nAlternative: developing bishops like Bf8-c5 puts pressure on f2, but ignores the knight on d7, which may be fatal.\n\nEven worse, if Black plays a flank pawn move, White can play Nxe5 or Nd7xb8, winning a rook.\n\nNd7xb8 is a direct threat — the rook on a8 is undefended!\n\nCurrently, the knight on d7 attacks b8 —