Nxb8**, Raxb8 — White wins rook for knight — +0 in material but positional damage.\n- But if Black plays **Kxd7**, then Black has captured a knight, and White has nothing — net **+1 knight** for Black.\n\nHuge difference.\n\nTherefore, **K: e8-d7** is forced and best.\n\nOther moves lose material.\n\nEven developing bishops or advancing pawns loses the rook or allows knight to escape with profit.\n\nOnly **K: e8-d7** and **R: a8-b8** avoid immediate material loss.\n\nBut **K: e8-d7** goes a step further — it **wins** material.\n\nTherefore, it is superior.\n\nNo other move offers such a clear advantage.\n\nReason: Capturing the knight on d7 with the king is necessary to prevent White from consolidating a dangerous outpost and to gain material. Moving the rook only avoids loss, while capturing gains a full piece. The king move is safe as the square is not attacked by any enemy piece (no White piece controls d7 except the knight itself), and it centralizes the king temporarily.\n\nAnswer: K: e8-d7'}
